Memory cap for the Apple packet tunnel: XHTTP's HTTP/2 receive window.

Under XHTTP the HTTP/2 library lets the server send 4 MiB ahead per stream
and up to 1 GiB per connection; Xray's own settings do not reach it. Held in
the extension, that alone outgrows its ~50 MiB (99 MiB measured with a slow
reader). This patch passes limits through net/http.HTTP2Config: 512 KiB per
stream, 4 MiB per connection (27 MiB measured).

Trade-off: one stream then carries at most ~70 Mbit/s at 60 ms RTT (~28 at
150 ms). UK and US run XHTTP only, so the per-stream figure must be tuned on
a real device before it ships.

NOT applied by default: it changes the engine of the iPhone build too, which
is the owner's decision. For a device test:

  bash scripts/build-libxray-tvos.sh \
      --extra-patch scripts/libxray/xray-core-apple-xhttp-h2-window.patch

LibXray.version then names it. If it ships, add it to build-libxray.sh and to
the `modifications` of Xray-core in scripts/libxray-notices.json (MPL-2.0).

